<Base slug="algorithms/memory/" title="Memory and changing taste" description="How the notebook remembers what someone likes, and how it notices when their life changes.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">04 · The algorithms · Stage 3</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>Remember slowly. Notice change quickly.</h1>
    <div class="headrow" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">
      <p class="lede muted">The notebook is what the cousin knows about you. It must not forget a real taste after one bad night, but it must notice when your life changes.</p>
      <Status s="tested" />
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">What the notebook is</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="h3">A short list of plain lines about you, that you can read.</p>
      <p>A real cousin doesn’t ask what you eat every time you meet. The notebook is how the app remembers between conversations. Without it, every request starts from zero and the app is just a search box. It is written in plain lines, not hidden scores, so you can open it, fix a line or delete it.</p>
      <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>What it keeps</th><th>Example lines</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Always</td><td>Tastes and needs that last</td><td>Vegetarian. Likes South Indian, calm places. Usually spends about ₹600. Hates long queues.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>These weeks</td><td>Things true for now, which expire</td><td>Early gym, up at 5. Trying new cuisines this month.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Today</td><td>Tonight only, cleared after</td><td>Tired, keep it close. A friend’s birthday.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recent outings</td><td>Where you went, with whom, how it went</td><td>Kaveri thali place, Friday, 4 friends: loved it.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p><b>Each line also records</b> where it came from (you said it, or it was guessed from outings), when, how many times it has been seen, how sure we are, and when it expires. What you say beats what we guessed, and today beats always.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Like human memory</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="h3">Built the way people remember, with two deliberate differences.</p>
      <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Human memory</th><th>Lasts</th><th>In the app</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Sensory, the moment</td><td>A few seconds</td><td>The message being read right now. The AI holds it only long enough to turn it into edits on the card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Short-term, working</td><td>Minutes</td><td>Today’s chat and the card: who is coming, what to find, the rules, tonight’s tastes. Cleared once the plan is chosen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Episodic: what happened, when, with whom</td><td>Weeks to years</td><td>Recent outings (“Kaveri thali place, Friday, 4 friends, loved it”), and the attic: old chats, searched only when you ask.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Semantic: what is true about you</td><td>Lasting</td><td>The notebook’s “always” page: vegetarian, likes South Indian, usually spends about ₹600.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Procedural: how to do things</td><td>Lasting</td><td>Not about you. The app’s own rules: fences, the mix, the route solver.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p><b>Consolidation.</b> People turn repeated events into facts: four late Fridays become “I’m a night person”. The app does the same through the next-day “how was it?”: outings slowly become notebook lines. The “these weeks” page sits in between, for things not yet known to last. If a line keeps holding, it moves to “always”; if not, it expires.</p>
      <p><b>Forgetting.</b> Lines expire and visited places rest, so one bad night doesn’t rewrite who you are.</p>
      <p><b>Where it differs on purpose.</b> Human memory quietly rewrites itself; the notebook must not. Every line says where it came from and how sure we are. And people can’t choose what they forget. Here you can read, fix or delete any line.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">How it fills and how it’s used</p>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="prose"><p class="h3">Filled from</p><ul>
        <li><b>A taste seed</b> on day one: a few “this or that” choices. In the simulation, this alone lifted first replies accepted from 57% to 70%.</li>
        <li><b>What you mention</b> in chat (“joined a gym, up at 5 now”).</li>
        <li><b>One question the next day</b>: “how was it?”</li>
        <li><b>A 20-second catch-up</b> once a month.</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="prose"><p class="h3">Read by</p><ul>
        <li><a href={url('algorithms/asking/')}>Asking</a>: anything already known is used silently, not asked.</li>
        <li><a href={url('algorithms/fences-and-magnets/')}>Fences and magnets</a>: needs become fences, tastes become magnets.</li>
        <li><a href={url('algorithms/rest-period/')}>Rest period</a>: recent outings rest before they come back.</li>
        <li><a href={url('algorithms/wild-cards/')}>Wild cards</a>: which kinds of surprise this person enjoys.</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Still open: exactly what is stored, for how long, and how export and deletion work. These need deciding before launch.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">In plain words</p>
    <div class="prose"><p>Every outing nudges the notebook a little. Nudges are small on purpose, so one bad evening can’t erase years of liking South Indian food. But that makes the notebook slow when life really changes: a new job, a new gym habit, a new baby. So there are three ways to catch a change, and they work best together.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Watch it work</p>
    <Video v="V07" />
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Three ways to catch a change</p>
    <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Way</th><th>How it works</th><th>Caught in the test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listen</td><td>A passing mention (“joined a gym, up at 5 now”) updates the notebook at once</td><td>82% of changes, about 2 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly catch-up</td><td>A 20 second card: “still into late nights?”</td><td>95%, about 1 week</td></tr><tr><td>Surprise meter</td><td>Counts ideas passed on in a row and brings the catch-up forward</td><td>31% alone, about 5 weeks late</td></tr><tr><td>Listen + catch-up</td><td>Both together; the meter, if used, only brings the catch-up forward</td><td>99.5%, about 1 week</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">For engineers</p>
    <details class="eng">
      <summary>The update rule</summary>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="muted">Numbers below are proposed starting values, to be tuned.</p>
        <pre class="code">notebook ← (1 − α)·notebook + α·outcome      α ≈ 0.08
mention → replace the line, flagged "said"
meter: passed_in_a_row ≥ 3 → move catch-up to next session</pre>
        <p>The notebook is an exponential moving average over tag vectors. In the drift simulation, the meter on its own raised many false alarms; using it only to move a catch-up forward avoids nagging.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
